{"type":"result","key":"v2:08aecc9b0337d9c7a4f2f32e5d8dc15f5e5a852a60d7f84e7c602e928167dbb4","agentId":"a3b47904c11c329d9","result":{"lens":"Supply chain and CI: the vendored daana-cli binary and its checksum pin (src/adss/engine.py, bin/linux-amd64/*), .github/workflows/ci.yml and source.yml, .pre-commit-config.yaml, pyproject.toml/uv.lock, and the engine's cwd=dab/ invocation.","findings":[],"consideredAndRejected":["CHECKSUM ENFORCEMENT ON EVERY EXECUTION PATH -- verified, no bypass. /home/user/adss-2026-09-07/src/adss/engine.py:79 Engine.run() calls require_detached() then self.verify() before subprocess.run([str(self.binary), ...]). grep across src/, tests/, destinations/ and checks/ finds exactly one construction of Engine (src/adss/cli.py:67) and no other invocation of the binary anywhere -- no test, no marimo cell, no CI step executes bin/linux-amd64/daana-cli outside Engine.run. All three dab subcommands (install/deploy/execute) and `adss build` route through it. verify() also fails closed on every degenerate input: missing .sha256 -> FileNotFoundError, empty file -> IndexError on .split()[0], uppercase digest -> mismatch. There is no `skip_verify` flag, no env-var escape and no cached-result short circuit. The newly added is_installed()/metadata_schema() early-return in dab_install (src/adss/cli.py:213-219) skips the *invocation*, never the verification of an invocation that happens.","CI CACHE POISONING OF THE ENGINE -- structurally closed, twice. .github/workflows/ci.yml restores `bin/linux-amd64/daana-cli` from actions/cache@v4 under key `engine-${{ steps.engine.outputs.sha256 }}`, which is untrusted storage. But the very next step (`Check the pin`) runs `(cd bin/linux-amd64 && sha256sum -c daana-cli.sha256)` against the *committed* checksum file, and the cache `path:` covers only the binary -- the .sha256 is never restored from the cache, so a cache entry cannot supply a matching checksum for a substituted binary. `adss build` then verifies a second time in Python. Separately, the trust boundary that would matter does not exist: GitHub scopes caches per ref; fork `pull_request` runs write to an isolated `refs/pull/N/merge` scope that base-branch runs cannot read, and a same-repo branch push already implies write access. Making the key content-addressed rather than version-addressed is the right shape.","GITHUB_OUTPUT INJECTION VIA THE PIN FILE -- no target. `echo \"sha256=$(cut -d' ' -f1 bin/linux-amd64/daana-cli.sha256)\" >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"` would let a multi-line .sha256 forge additional step outputs. But (a) command substitution output is not re-evaluated as shell, so there is no command injection; (b) .sha256 is committed repository content, trusted per the stated model; (c) nothing in the job reads any output of that step except the cache key, and the cache key is then re-validated by sha256sum -c. Even a successful injection buys nothing.","TOCTOU BETWEEN verify() AND subprocess.run -- no privilege boundary crossed. src/adss/engine.py:79-83 hashes the file, closes it, then execs the path. Swapping the file in that window requires write access to the working tree, which is the same access that would let you edit engine.py itself, or Project.engine_binary, or any of the SQL the build runs. In CI the workspace is not shared with any other principal. Fixing this (exec via an open fd) would defend against nobody who is not already inside.","NON-CONSTANT-TIME CHECKSUM COMPARISON -- irrelevant by construction. `if digest != recorded` (src/adss/engine.py:71) compares two values that are both public: `recorded` is a plaintext file committed to the repository and mirrored in the git-LFS pointer. There is no secret, no remote attacker and no oracle to time -- a timing side channel would leak a value anyone can `cat`.","THE PIN PROVES INTEGRITY, NOT PROVENANCE -- real, but not an exploit, and already documented. verify() reads the expected digest from `self.binary.parent / f'{name}.sha256'`, i.e. from a file sitting